Mac distribution layer
======================

This layer depends on OE-Core (Wrynose). The project kas configuration selects
it with the Mac BSP and linux-stable for both images.

* mac-image is the OS for the internal SSD and the source WIC for installation.
* mac-image-installer is the bootable USB rescue image. It adds disk utilities
  and /usr/sbin/install-macbookpro14-3 to the same base system.

Headless wired access
---------------------

Both images install mac-debug-user, systemd-networkd and systemd-conf, and
selects OpenSSH through ssh-server-openssh. No additional network manager or
DHCP client is needed.

* OE-Core's unmodified systemd-conf recipe installs
  /usr/lib/systemd/network/80-wired.network through its default
  dhcp-ethernet PACKAGECONFIG. Type=ether matches Ethernet interfaces and
  DHCP=yes enables IPv4 and IPv6 DHCP. The policy excludes veth* interfaces
  and does not apply when the kernel command line configures networking
  through nfsroot or ip. It also uses the DHCP-provided MTU, route metric 10
  and a MAC-based client identifier. No custom wired policy is installed.
* OE-Core's default systemd PACKAGECONFIG includes networkd, building and
  packaging it as systemd-networkd. mac-image explicitly installs that
  package. OE-Core declares systemd-networkd.service in SYSTEMD_SERVICE for
  the package, and systemd.bbclass defaults SYSTEMD_AUTO_ENABLE to enable,
  providing automatic service startup without a project systemd append.
* OE-Core's default systemd-sshd-socket-mode PACKAGECONFIG registers
  sshd.socket, which systemd.bbclass enables by default. The socket listens
  on port 22 and starts sshd@.service instances for incoming connections.
  sshd.socket pulls in sshdgenkeys.service through Wants=, and sshd@.service
  orders each instance after key generation. Device-specific host keys are
  generated automatically; no host private keys are shipped by this layer.
  openssh_%.bbappend only installs and packages the key-only SSH policy.
* 00-mac-key-only.conf is included before other settings by OE's sshd_config.
  It requires public-key authentication and disables passwords,
  keyboard-interactive authentication, empty passwords and root SSH login.
* mac-debug-user.bb uses useradd to create the normal user and group ming,
  with /home/ming and /bin/sh (provided by the base image). The password
  field is the deliberately unmatchable marker '*NP*'. It is neither an
  empty password nor a leading '!' account lock: a locked account would also
  block key login with this distribution's non-PAM OpenSSH configuration.
  The account is created during image assembly, without a first-login step.
* The existing recipes-core/users/files/ming.pub is installed verbatim as
  /home/ming/.ssh/authorized_keys. The home, .ssh directory and key file are
  owned by ming:ming with modes 0750, 0700 and 0600 respectively. Replace
  the public key in version control and rebuild to provision another key.
  Keep the corresponding private key on the SSH client.

Root SSH is disabled. The configured ming account has passwordless sudo
through the existing /etc/sudoers.d/90-ming policy.
The kernel, graphics settings and GRUB configuration are unchanged.

Build and validation
--------------------

From the repository root, use the existing kas configuration:

    kas shell kas/main.yaml:kas/local.yaml -c 'bitbake -p'
    kas shell kas/main.yaml:kas/local.yaml -c 'bitbake-layers show-appends'
    kas shell kas/main.yaml:kas/local.yaml -c 'bitbake -n mac-image mac-image-installer'
    kas build kas/main.yaml:kas/local.yaml --target mac-image --target mac-image-installer

Find both .wic.zst files and their .wic.bmap files under
build/tmp/deploy/images/macbookpro14-3/.

Installing from USB
-------------------

1. Back up any other data you need from the SSD. The installer automatically
   preserves EFI/APPLE from the existing EFI partition.
2. Write the mac-image-installer WIC image to a dedicated rescue USB drive.
   Use an image writer that supports .wic.zst, or decompress it first. This
   erases the selected USB drive. Start the Mac while holding Option and
   select the rescue drive's EFI Boot entry.
3. Place the mac-image .wic or .wic.zst on storage accessible from the rescue
   system and separate from /dev/nvme0n1. Confirm with lsblk that it is the intended internal
   4Kn SSD. Unmount its volumes and deactivate any swap on it first.
4. Run the dry-run command, then repeat without --dry-run after reviewing
   its output. The real run validates the WIC filesystems and checks the
   saved Apple EFI files before asking you to type ERASE.

       sudo /usr/sbin/install-macbookpro14-3 --dry-run \
           /mnt/data/mac-image-macbookpro14-3.rootfs.wic.zst \
           /dev/nvme0n1

   Dry-run mounts existing SSD EFI partitions read-only and reports the
   backup source and destination. It leaves the SSD untouched and creates
   no Apple EFI backup. Both modes decompress compressed input into a temporary
   file. If /tmp is too small for the uncompressed WIC, point TMPDIR to a writable
   directory on separate storage using sudo env TMPDIR=/mnt/data/tmp.
5. Shut down after a successful install, remove the rescue USB, and boot
   from the internal SSD. The installer restores and verifies EFI/APPLE,
   installs the fallback EFI bootloader, and sets GRUB and /boot to use the
   new partition UUIDs. It leaves EFI NVRAM unchanged.

Apple EFI backup and recovery
-----------------------------

Use --backup-dir /mnt/data/backups to choose an existing writable destination
on separate persistent storage. Otherwise the installer looks alongside the
WIC, in TMPDIR and on the rescue system, and checks mounted media under /mnt,
/media and /run/media. It prints the path of the verified mac-apple-efi.*
directory and keeps persistent copies for recovery.

When only RAM is available, the real run requires you to type RAM BACKUP
before the separate ERASE confirmation. Power loss or reboot can destroy the
RAM copy. If installation fails after SSD writes begin, the installer keeps
that copy in RAM. Save it to persistent media before restarting or rebooting.

If the SSD has no Apple files, pass a persistent backup directory containing
APPLE as the final argument. This can be a mac-apple-efi.* directory from an
earlier run or a copy of the original EFI/APPLE tree. Keep it outside the
repository and images.

    sudo /usr/sbin/install-macbookpro14-3 \
        /mnt/data/mac-image-macbookpro14-3.rootfs.wic.zst \
        /dev/nvme0n1 /mnt/data/mac-apple-efi.XXXXXX

If the EFI partition cannot be mounted read-only or multiple EFI partitions
contain Apple files, resolve the discovery error before installing.

Inspecting networking and SSH
-----------------------------

After building, inspect the image manifest and rootfs before booting:

    kas shell kas/main.yaml:kas/local.yaml -c 'bitbake -e mac-image' > /tmp/mac-image.env

Use IMAGE_ROOTFS from that output to check:

* /etc/passwd: ming has /home/ming and an executable /bin/sh.
* /etc/shadow: ming has '*NP*', with no account/password expiry requiring
  interactive password changes.
* /home/ming, .ssh and authorized_keys: ownership and modes as listed above;
  compare authorized_keys with the source public key.
* /usr/lib/systemd/network/80-wired.network: OE-Core's default wired policy
  with DHCP=yes; no custom 20-wired.network should override it.
* Enablement links/presets for systemd-networkd.service and sshd.socket,
  plus the packaged sshd@.service and sshdgenkeys.service units and their
  key-generation dependencies/order.
* /etc/ssh/sshd_config includes sshd_config.d/*.conf before other settings,
  and that directory contains 00-mac-key-only.conf.

First boot requires a writable root filesystem for generated host keys,
a supported wired adapter with carrier, a reachable DHCP server, and a
network path allowing TCP port 22. USB/Thunderbolt Ethernet enumeration,
driver support and actual key login must be confirmed on the MacBook.
No interaction with its screen or keyboard is needed for provisioning.
Find the IPv4 address in the DHCP server/router's lease list, then run on
the client (substitute the matching private-key path and leased address):

    ssh -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -i /path/to/private-key ming@MAC_IP

Accept/verify the newly generated host key on the client as appropriate.
After connecting:

    id
    echo "$HOME $SHELL"
    ip -4 address
    networkctl status
    systemctl is-enabled systemd-networkd.service sshd.socket
    systemctl is-active systemd-networkd.service sshd.socket
    stat -c '%U:%G %a %n' /home/ming /home/ming/.ssh /home/ming/.ssh/authorized_keys

From the client, verify password-only and root connections fail:

    ssh -o PubkeyAuthentication=no -o PreferredAuthentications=password,keyboard-interactive ming@MAC_IP
    ssh -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -i /path/to/private-key root@MAC_IP

An image build and hardware boot are required to verify final rootfs
ownership, service startup, host-key generation, DHCP leases and SSH login.
Metadata parsing and dry runs alone cannot establish those runtime results.
